      "searchText": "Electric Fields in Matter - Topics Home > Electric Fields in Matter author Turin Prepnik Electric Fields in Matter So far, we have studied electric fields in a vacuum. In this section, we explore how matter influences electrostatics. Matter comes in two main varieties: conductors and dielectrics (insulators). We will examine how external fields induce charges on conductors or polarize the atoms within insulators. This leads to the concept of bound charges and necessitates the introduction of a new auxiliary field, the Electric Displacement (D) . Conductors Basic properties, induced charges, surface charge density, and cavities. → Polarization Induced dipoles, alignment of polar molecules, and the polarization vector P. → The Field of a Polarized Object Bound surface and volume charges, and the physical interpretation of polarization. → The Electric Displacement Gauss's Law in the presence of dielectrics and the auxiliary field D. → Linear Dielectrics Susceptibility, permittivity, dielectric constants, and boundary value problems. →",
