      "searchText": "Magnetostatics - Topics Home > Magnetostatics author Turin Prepnik Magnetostatics While electrostatics deals with stationary charges, Magnetostatics deals with stationary currents (steady flow of charge). Here, the electric field is constant, but the moving charges generate a Magnetic Field (B) . The fundamental laws governing this regime are the Biot-Savart Law (analogous to Coulomb's Law) and Ampère's Law (analogous to Gauss's Law). We also introduce the Vector Potential A , which plays a mathematical role similar to the scalar potential V. The Lorentz Force Law Defining the magnetic field B via the force on a moving charge and the nature of currents. → The Biot-Savart Law Calculating the magnetic field produced by steady line, surface, and volume currents. → The Divergence and Curl of B Ampère's Law, the absence of magnetic monopoles, and applications to symmetric systems. → Magnetic Vector Potential Introducing the vector potential A, gauge freedom, and the multipole expansion. →",
