      "searchText": "Magnetic Fields in Matter - Topics Home > Magnetic Fields in Matter author Turin Prepnik Magnetic Fields in Matter Just as electric fields polarize matter, magnetic fields induce Magnetization . However, the response of materials to magnetic fields is more varied and complex than in electrostatics. We classify materials into three main types: Diamagnets (which oppose the field), Paramagnets (which align with the field), and Ferromagnets (which retain magnetization). To handle these effects macroscopically, we introduce the auxiliary field H and the concept of bound currents. Magnetization Diamagnets, Paramagnets, Ferromagnets, and the definition of Magnetization (M). → The Field of a Magnetized Object Calculating the field produced by magnetization using Bound Currents. → The Auxiliary Field H Introducing the H-field to simplify problems involving magnetic materials. → Linear and Nonlinear Media Magnetic susceptibility, permeability, and the hysteresis of ferromagnets. →",
