      "searchText": "Electric Potential Home > Electrostatics > Electric Potential Electric Potential The Electric Field \\(\\vec{E}\\) is a vector quantity, which makes it computationally expensive to handle. To simplify the analysis of electrostatics, we introduce a scalar field: the Electric Potential (\\(V\\)) . The useful idea is that \\(V\\) stores the same electrostatic information in a scalar form. Once we know the potential at two points, we can compare them directly: moving a charge \\(Q\\) from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\) involves the potential difference \\(\\Delta V = V_b - V_a\\). The electric field does work \\(W_{\\text{field}} = Q(V_a - V_b)\\), so in many problems we can avoid following the full vector field along the path and just compare the endpoint potentials. 1. Definition and The Curl The definition of potential relies entirely on a specific property of the electrostatic field. Because the field is conservative, its curl is zero everywhere. $$ \\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = 0 $$ A vector field with zero curl can always be written as the gradient of a scalar. This allows us to define \\(V\\) such that: $$ \\vec{E} = - \\nabla V $$ To find the potential at a specific point \\(\\vec{r}\\), we integrate the field from a reference point \\(\\mathcal{O}\\): $$ V(\\vec{r}) = - \\int_{\\mathcal{O}}^{\\vec{r}} \\vec{E} \\cdot d\\vec{l} $$ What is \\(\\mathcal{O}\\)? The symbol \\(\\mathcal{O}\\) represents the standard reference point where we agree that \\(V=0\\). For localized charge distributions (like a single atom or a charged ball), we typically place this reference point at infinity (\\(\\infty\\)). + Deep Dive: Derivation & The Curl Condition The simulator below visualizes \\(V\\) as a color map. Red means positive potential, blue means negative potential, and black means the potential is close to zero. Brighter color means a large",
