      "searchText": "Work and Energy - Physics II Home > Electrostatics > Work & Energy Work and Energy How much energy is stored in an electrostatic system? We can answer this by calculating the work required to assemble the system, bringing charges in from infinity one by one. 1. Work to Move a Charge Based on the definition of electric potential, the work \\(W\\) required to move a test charge \\(Q\\) from point \\(a\\) to point \\(b\\) is simply the charge times the potential difference. $$ W = Q [V(\\vec{b}) - V(\\vec{a})] $$ If we bring the charge in from infinity (where \\(V=0\\)) to a point \\(\\vec{r}\\), the work done is: $$ W = Q V(\\vec{r}) $$ 2. The Energy of Point Charges Imagine assembling a system of point charges from scratch. First charge (\\(q_1\\)): Takes 0 work (no field yet). Second charge (\\(q_2\\)): Must move against the field of \\(q_1\\). Work is \\(q_2 V_1(\\vec{r}_2)\\). Third charge (\\(q_3\\)): Must move against fields of \\(q_1\\) and \\(q_2\\). Summing the work for all charges gives the total energy of the system. To avoid double-counting pairs, we divide by 2: $$ W = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i=1}^n q_i V(\\vec{r}_i) $$ Where \\(V(\\vec{r}_i)\\) is the potential at the position of charge \\(i\\) caused by all other charges. + Deep Dive: Point Charge Assembly 3. Continuous Charge Distributions For a continuous distribution (like a charged cloud or solid object), the sum becomes an integral. We replace \\(q_i\\) with \\(\\rho d\\tau\\) and the summation with integration over the volume. $$ W = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{\\mathcal{V}} \\rho V d\\tau $$ This equation represents the energy stored in the configuration of the charge density \\(\\rho\\). 4. Where is the Energy Stored? Using Gauss's Law (\\(\\rho = \\epsilon_0 \\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E}\\)) and integration by parts, we can transform the equation above into one involving onl",
