      "searchText": "Multipole Expansion Home > Electrostatics > Multipole Expansion Multipole Expansion When calculating the potential of a localized charge distribution from a significant distance ($r \\gg r'$), it is often inefficient to perform the full integration. Instead, we expand the potential into a series of terms ordered by powers of $1/r$. This series is called the Multipole Expansion . 1. Approximate Potentials at Large Distances Consider a charge distribution $\\rho(\\mathbf{r}')$. The exact potential at point $\\mathbf{r}$ is given by: $$ V(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\int \\frac{1}{\\rsep} \\rho(\\mathbf{r}') d\\tau' $$ As derived in the deep-dive, we can expand $1/\\rsep$ using Legendre Polynomials. The resulting potential is a sum: $$ V(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{r^{n+1}} \\int (r')^n P_n(\\cos\\alpha) \\rho(\\mathbf{r}') d\\tau' $$ $$ V(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\left[ \\frac{1}{r} \\int \\rho d\\tau' + \\frac{1}{r^2} \\int r' \\cos\\alpha \\rho d\\tau' + \\dots \\right] $$ $$ = \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{Q}{r}}_{\\text{Monopole}} + \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{\\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{r}}}{r^2}}_{\\text{Dipole}} + \\underbrace{\\dots}_{\\text{Quadrupole}} $$ Each term in this sum corresponds to a different multipole moment . The expansion allows us to classify complex distributions by their dominant behavior at long distances. 2. The Multipole Hierarchy Usually, we are only interested in the first few terms. As $n$ increases, the terms die off faster with distance. Monopole (n=0) $V_{mon} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{Q}{r}$ The dominant term if the object has a net total charge $Q$. At large distances, any charged object looks like a point charge. Dipole (n=1) $V_{dip} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\fra",
