          "title": "0. Conductors and Dielectrics",
          "searchText": "0. Conductors and Dielectrics A conductor is a material, usually a metal such as copper, aluminum, or iron, in which some electrons can move through the object. A dielectric, also called an insulator, is a material such as plastic, glass, rubber, or dry wood. In a dielectric, charge cannot flow freely through the whole object. It can only shift a little inside atoms or molecules, which is studied in the next lesson. A neutral conductor is made of atoms: positive nuclei stay in place as part of the solid, while some electrons can move from place to place. Positive and negative charge balance in each small region, so the net charge of the interior is zero and the whole conductor has no net charge unless extra charge is added or induced. The material response simulator uses dielectric and conductor modes with a smooth animated transition. Dielectric mode shows local shifting: electrons remain near their own atoms and make small dipoles. Conductor mode shows long-range motion: electrons can move across the object, so an outside field can create charge imbalance near the surfaces. The surface statement belongs to the next concept and is about net excess charge, not every electron leaving the inside.",
