          "searchText": "1. The Phenomenon: Forces on Moving Charges In electrostatics, we learned that a stationary charge q produces an electric field , and another charge Q feels a force = Q . This is simple and intuitive. But what happens when charges move? Consider two parallel wires. The wires are electrically neutral (they have equal amounts of positive and negative charge), so there should be no electrostatic force between them. Yet, when we run a current through them, they physically move. If the currents flow in the same direction, the wires attract. If they flow in opposite directions, they repel. Since the net charge is zero, this cannot be an electric force. It is a Magnetic Force .",
