          "title": "2. The \"Chicken and Egg\" Problem",
          "searchText": "2. The \"Chicken and Egg\" Problem We know a force exists, but how do we describe the field that causes it? In gravity and electrostatics, the force vector points in the direction of the field lines. Magnetism is not so kind. We cannot simply define the magnetic field by placing a stationary charge and measuring the force, because stationary charges feel no magnetic force . The charge must be moving. Operational Definition: Probing the Field Imagine we are in a room with a hidden magnetic field. We have a \"test charge\" q and a gun to fire it at velocity . How do we find ? If we fire the charge along the direction of the field, we observe zero force . This tells us the direction of . If we fire the charge perpendicular to that line, we observe maximum force . This allows us to calculate the magnitude. Crucially, the force is always perpendicular to both the velocity and the field. Use the simulation below to act as the experimenter. Try to deduce the hidden field direction by firing particles.",
