      "searchText": "Divergence, Curl & Comparison Home > Magnetostatics > Divergence, Curl & Ampère's Law Divergence, Curl, and Ampère's Law 1. The Divergence of B The behavior of magnetic fields regarding \"sources\" and \"sinks\" is described by the divergence. Unlike electric fields, which diverge from positive charges, magnetic fields are solenoidal . $$ \\underbrace{\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0}_{\\text{Differential Form}} \\quad \\longleftrightarrow \\quad \\underbrace{\\oint_{\\mathcal{S}} \\mathbf{B} \\cdot d\\mathbf{a} = 0}_{\\text{Integral Form}} $$ Physical Interpretation: No Magnetic Monopoles: There is no magnetic equivalent to electric charge ($q$). You cannot isolate a \"North\" pole. Closed Loops: Magnetic field lines have no beginning or end; they form continuous closed loops. Flux Conservation: The net magnetic flux entering any closed volume must exactly equal the flux leaving it. 2. The Curl of B (Ampère's Law) While the divergence of $\\mathbf{B}$ is zero, the curl is non-zero wherever current flows. This describes the rotational or \"circulating\" nature of the magnetic field. $$ \\underbrace{\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0 \\mathbf{J}}_{\\text{Differential Form}} \\quad \\longleftrightarrow \\quad \\underbrace{\\oint_{\\mathcal{P}} \\mathbf{B} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\mu_0 I_{\\text{enc}}}_{\\text{Integral Form}} $$ Physical Interpretation: Circulation: Electric currents ($\\mathbf{J}$ or $I$) act as vortices that cause the magnetic field to curl around them. Calculation Tool: The integral form (Ampère's Law) allows us to calculate $\\mathbf{B}$ easily when the system possesses high symmetry (cylindrical, planar, or solenoidal), allowing us to pull $|\\mathbf{B}|$ out of the integral. + Deep Dive: Applications of Ampere's Law 3. Comparison: Magnetostatics vs. Electrostatics The theories of static electric ",
