      "searchText": "Magnetization Home > Fields in Matter > Magnetization Magnetization If you ask the average person what \"magnetism\" is, they will likely talk about refrigerator magnets or compass needles—things that seem to have no obvious connection to moving charges. Yet, all magnetic phenomena are ultimately due to electric charges in motion. If you look at matter on an atomic scale, you find tiny currents: electrons orbiting nuclei and spinning on their axes. These microscopic current loops act as magnetic dipoles. When an external magnetic field is applied, these dipoles align or shift, causing the material to become magnetized . 1. Diamagnets, Paramagnets, Ferromagnets Unlike electric polarization, where induced dipoles almost always line up with the electric field, magnetic response is more varied. We classify materials based on how their internal dipoles respond to an external field $\\mathbf{B}$: I. Paramagnets: Materials containing unpaired electrons (like Aluminum or Oxygen). Their atomic dipoles try to align parallel to the external field. However, they are constantly jostled by thermal energy, so the alignment is usually very weak. They are weakly attracted to magnets. II. Diamagnets: Materials where the orbital motion of electrons shifts to create a field opposite to the external field. This effect is present in all matter (like Water or Copper) but is usually masked if paramagnetism is present. They are weakly repelled by magnets. III. Ferromagnets: Materials like Iron where quantum mechanical effects lock neighboring dipoles together into \"domains.\" They retain their magnetization even after the external field is removed. Note: Ferromagnetism is theoretically more complex than the other two. We will focus on Paramagnetism and Diamagnetism here, and return to Ferromagnets ",
      "snippet": "Magnetization Home > Fields in Matter > Magnetization Magnetization If you ask the average person what \"magnetism\" is, they will likely talk about refrigerator magnets or compass needles—things that seem to have no obvio"
