      "searchText": "The Field of a Magnetized Object Home > Fields in Matter > Field of a Magnetized Object The Field of a Magnetized Object In the previous section, we defined magnetization \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) as the magnetic dipole moment per unit volume. But how do we actually calculate the magnetic field \\(\\mathbf{B}\\) produced by an object with a given magnetization? Just as polarization \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) effectively accumulates charge, magnetization \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) effectively creates currents. We call these Bound Currents . 1. Bound Currents Suppose we have a chunk of magnetized material. The total vector potential \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) at an observation point is the sum of the potentials produced by all the infinitesimal dipoles in the volume. Through mathematical transformation (integration by parts), we discover that the potential produced by magnetization is exactly the same as the potential produced by a specific volume current density \\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\) and a surface current density \\(\\mathbf{K}_b\\). $$ \\mathbf{J}_b = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M} $$ $$ \\mathbf{K}_b = \\mathbf{M} \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} $$ Here, \\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\) acts throughout the body of the material, while \\(\\mathbf{K}_b\\) flows along the surface (where \\(\\mathbf{\\hat{n}}\\) is the surface normal). ⚡ Electrostatic Analogy Recall that for a polarized object, we defined Bound Charges : $$ \\rho_b = -\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{P} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sigma_b = \\mathbf{P} \\cdot \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} $$ Electric polarization acts like a pile-up of charge (divergence), whereas magnetization acts like a circulation of current (curl). + Deep Dive: Derivation & Example 2. Physical Interpretation Are these \"bound currents\" real? Yes. They represent the cumulative effect of the microscopic atomic current loops. Imagine a uniform grid of tiny current l",
