          "id": "concept-3-3-boundary-conditions",
          "title": "3. Boundary Conditions",
          "searchText": "3. Boundary Conditions Just as we did for electrostatics, we must determine how fields change when crossing the interface between two materials (or vacuum and matter). We use the integral forms of the divergence and curl laws. Field Component Electric Boundary Condition Magnetic Boundary Condition Perpendicular (Discontinuous by free charge) (Always Continuous! No magnetic monopoles) Parallel (Always Continuous) (Discontinuous by free surface current) Notice the symmetry breaking here. For electric fields, the fundamental field is continuous parallel to the surface. For magnetic fields, the fundamental field is continuous perpendicular to the surface. However, notice that is discontinuous if there is a discontinuity in Magnetization ( ), acting like a \"magnetic surface charge.\" + Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions & Shielding",
