          "title": "3. Ferromagnetism: Domains & Magnetization",
          "searchText": "3. Ferromagnetism: Domains & Magnetization Some materials, most notably Iron, Nickel, and Cobalt, are dramatically different. They are Nonlinear and can become permanent magnets . Why Isn't Every Piece of Iron a Magnet? In a ferromagnet, atomic dipoles spontaneously align with one another due to quantum-mechanical \"exchange coupling\"—even without an external field. If all the dipoles in a piece of iron were aligned, it would produce a strong magnetic field . But in practice, the alignment occurs only in small patches called domains . Within each domain, billions of dipoles point in the same direction. However, different domains point in different directions . Their magnetic fields largely cancel, so an ordinary piece of iron appears unmagnetized. Zoom in to reveal the aligned atomic dipoles responsible for the magnet's field. How to Magnetize a Material To turn an ordinary piece of iron into a permanent magnet, you must align the domains. This is done by applying a strong external magnetic field . When is applied: Domains aligned with the field grow at the expense of unfavorably aligned domains. Domain walls shift. As increases further, domains rotate to align more closely with the field. At very high fields, the material reaches saturation —nearly all dipoles point the same way, and the material produces a strong net . 🧲 The Birth of a Permanent Magnet Crucially, when you turn off the external field ( ), the domain walls don't fully return to their original positions. Some alignment remains . This residual magnetization is called Remanence . The material now produces its own field even without an external source—it has become a permanent magnet . Hysteresis: The Memory Effect This irreversibility is called hysteresis . If you plot vs , you don't get a straight line. Instead, the path going \"up\" (increasing ) differs from the path going \"down\" (decreasing ). The curve forms a loop. B depends on the history of H, not just its current value. + Deep Dive: Ferromagnetism: Domains & Hysteresis",
