      "searchText": "Linear and Nonlinear Media Home > Fields in Matter > Linear & Nonlinear Media Linear and Nonlinear Media We have defined the magnetization \\(\\bfM\\) and the auxiliary field \\(\\bfH\\). In practical engineering and physics, the crucial question is: How does a specific material respond when I apply a magnetic field? Does the material boost the field? Does it fight against it? Or does it do something more complex, like remembering its past state? This distinction divides materials into Linear and Nonlinear media. 1. Magnetic Susceptibility and Permeability For an enormous class of materials (diamagnets and paramagnets), the magnetization \\(\\bfM\\) is directly proportional to the applied auxiliary field \\(\\bfH\\). These are called Linear Media . We quantify this relationship with a dimensionless constant \\(\\chi_m\\), called the Magnetic Susceptibility : $$ \\bfM = \\chi_m \\bfH $$ This linear relationship allows us to rewrite the total magnetic field \\(\\bfB\\) in terms of \\(\\bfH\\) alone. Since \\(\\bfB = \\mu_0(\\bfH + \\bfM)\\), we get: $$ \\bfB = \\mu_0(1 + \\chi_m)\\bfH = \\mu \\bfH $$ Here, \\(\\mu\\) is the Permeability of the material. It encompasses the total magnetic response of the medium. ⚡ Electrostatic Analogy Electric: \\(\\bfP = \\epsilon_0 \\chi_e \\bfE\\) and \\(\\bfD = \\epsilon \\bfE\\). In dielectrics, \\(\\chi_e\\) is always positive (material aligns with field). Magnetic: \\(\\bfM = \\chi_m \\bfH\\) and \\(\\bfB = \\mu \\bfH\\). Here, \\(\\chi_m\\) can be positive (Paramagnetic) OR negative (Diamagnetic). This simple proportionality makes solving boundary value problems much easier, as the complex microscopic currents are hidden inside the constant \\(\\mu\\). + Deep Dive: The Filled Solenoid + Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions & Shielding 2. Temperature Dependence The magnetic behavior of materials is strongl",
