      "searchText": "Ch 7.3: Maxwell's Equations Home > Electrodynamics > Maxwell's Equations Maxwell's Equations This section summarizes the complete set of four equations that govern classical electrodynamics, uniting electricity, magnetism, and electromagnetic waves. 1. The Complete Set Electrodynamics is governed by four partial differential equations (Maxwell's) and one force law (Lorentz). Together, they describe everything from light waves to electric motors. The Force Law This tells us how fields affect charges: $$ \\vec{F} = q(\\vec{E} + \\vec{v} \\times \\vec{B}) $$ 2. The Ampère-Maxwell Correction In steady circuits, current flows in a complete loop. But consider a charging capacitor : current flows into one plate and out of the other, but nothing (no charges) physically crosses the gap. This creates a paradox: if there is no current in the gap, Ampère's Law says there should be no magnetic field there. But experiments show the field does exist. Maxwell realized that as the plates charge, the Electric Field between them is changing , and this change acts exactly like a current. The Full Ampère-Maxwell Law Maxwell's correction to Ampère's Law, incorporating the displacement current density ($J_d$): $$ \\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\vec{J} + \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t} $$ Where the term $\\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t}$ is the Displacement Current . + Deep Dive: How Maxwell Fixed Ampère's Law 3. The General Equations (Vacuum) In free space, these are the fundamental relationships. $$ \\begin{align} \\text{(i)} \\quad & \\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E} = \\frac{\\rho}{\\epsilon_0} \\\\ \\text{(ii)} \\quad & \\nabla \\cdot \\vec{B} = 0 \\\\ \\text{(iii)} \\quad & \\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = -\\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t} \\\\ \\text{(iv)} \\quad & \\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\v",
