      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Atomic Picture of Charge Home > Electrostatics > The Electric Field > Atomic Picture of Charge Deep Dive: Atomic Picture of Charge The main Electric Field lesson gives the essential meaning of charge. This deep dive slows down the atomic picture: which particles carry charge, when an atom becomes charged, and why a whole atom or object can be treated as charged. 1. The Fundamental Property Charge is a fundamental property of matter, much like mass. While mass determines how an object responds to gravity, charge determines how it interacts with electromagnetic fields . There are two types of charge: Positive (+) and Negative (-) . As Benjamin Franklin famously discovered, like charges repel, and opposite charges attract. 2. The Subatomic Particles In the macroscopic world, charge seems continuous. But on an atomic level, it is carried by discrete particles. The atom consists of a dense nucleus containing Protons and Neutrons , surrounded by a cloud of Electrons . Particle Charge (C) Mass (kg) Proton \\(+1.602 \\times 10^{-19}\\) \\(1.673 \\times 10^{-27}\\) Electron \\(-1.602 \\times 10^{-19}\\) \\(9.110 \\times 10^{-31}\\) Neutron \\(0\\) \\(1.675 \\times 10^{-27}\\) Interesting Fact: Protons and Neutrons are about 1,840 times heavier than Electrons! Despite this massive difference in mass, the Proton and Electron have exactly the same magnitude of electric charge. 3. When Does an Atom Become Charged? A normal atom is usually neutral because its number of protons equals its number of electrons. The positive charge of the nucleus and the negative charge of the electron cloud cancel: \\(q_{\\text{atom}} = (+N_p e) + (-N_e e) = (N_p - N_e)e\\) Here \\(N_p\\) is the number of protons and \\(N_e\\) is the number of electrons. In the simulator below, the model atom starts with 6 protons, ",
