      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Coulomb's Law Home > Electrostatics > Coulomb's Law author Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: Coulomb's Law The Fundamental Law of Force In 1785, Charles Coulomb published the results of a series of delicate experiments using a torsion balance. He discovered that the force between two stationary point charges is: $$ \\vec{F} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2} \\hat{r} $$ This equation contains several critical pieces of information: Magnitude: The force is proportional to the product of the charges ($q_1 q_2$) and inversely proportional to the square of the distance ($r^2$). Direction ($\\hat{r}$): The force acts along the line joining the two charges. If $q_1 q_2 > 0$ (like charges), the force is repulsive. If $q_1 q_2 Point Charges: Strictly speaking, this law applies only to point charges. For extended objects, we must use integration (superposition). Interactive Simulator Explore the relationship between charge, distance, and force. Drag the charges to move them. Charge 1 ($q_1$): 10 μC Charge 2 ($q_2$): -10 μC Distance ($r$): ... F = ... 🧪 Experiments to Try Inverse Square Law: Place the charges at a specific distance (e.g., 4 grid squares). Note the force. Move them to half that distance (2 squares). Does the force quadruple? Linearity of Charge: Keep the distance constant. Double the value of $q_1$. Does the force vector exactly double in length? Zero Force: Set one of the charges to 0 μC. The force vanishes, confirming that interaction requires two parties. Why the Weird Constant? ($1/4\\pi\\epsilon_0$) You might wonder why we write the constant as $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}$ instead of just a simple $k$. The factor $4\\pi$ is purely geometric. It comes from the surface area of a sphere ($4\\pi r^2$). Because the electric influence spreads out spherically fro",
