      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Coulomb's Law Home > Electrostatics > The Electric Field > Coulomb's Law Deep Dive: Coulomb's Law Interactive Laboratory Before diving into the mathematical nuances, explore the physical behavior of charges using the simulator below. Observe how the force vector changes as you manipulate the magnitude of the charges and the distance between them. The notation is the same as in the main lesson: the source charge \\(q\\) is at \\(\\vec{r}_q\\), the test charge \\(Q\\) is at \\(\\vec{r}_Q\\), and the separation vector is \\(\\vecrsep = \\vec{r}_Q - \\vec{r}_q\\). We introduce \\(\\vecrsep\\) because positions alone do not yet give the force direction; subtracting them gives the vector from \\(q\\) to \\(Q\\), and \\(\\hatrsep\\) is the unit vector in that direction. Physical Limits & Behavior The Idea of Proportionality Coulomb's Law is more than just a calculation; it is a statement of proportionality . Specifically, it tells us that: The force is jointly proportional to the product of the charges ($F \\propto qQ$). Doubling one charge doubles the force; doubling both quadruples it. The force is inversely proportional to the square of the separation length ($F \\propto 1/|\\vecrsep|^2$). This \"Inverse Square Law\" is a hallmark of physical phenomena that spread out from a point source into three-dimensional space. The sign of the product also gives the direction. If the charges have the same sign, then \\(qQ > 0\\), so the force points in the same direction as \\(\\hatrsep\\). Since \\(\\hatrsep\\) points from the source charge toward the test charge, the test charge is pushed away: like charges repel. If the signs are opposite, then \\(qQ What happens as $\\rsep \\to \\infty$? As the distance between the charges increases, the denominator $|\\vecrsep|^2$ grows very large, causing the force $F$ to appr",
