      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Electric Field Visualizations Home > Electrostatics > The Electric Field > Field Visualizations Deep Dive: Electric Field Visualizations Before we dive into complex integrals, we must build a strong mental model. How does one charge \"know\" that another charge is nearby? Does it reach out with invisible arms? 1. What is the Electric Field? Action at a Distance vs. Field Theory Instead of saying \"Charge A pushes Charge B directly,\" we adopt the Field perspective: Charge A modifies the space around it, creating an Electric Field ($\\vec{E}$) . Charge B sits in this field and feels a force $\\vec{F}$ exerted by the field at that specific location. Mathematically, the electric field is simply the force per unit charge . If you place a tiny \"test charge\" $Q$ at a point, the field is defined as: $$ \\vec{E} = \\frac{\\vec{F}}{Q} $$ (Units: Newtons per Coulomb, N/C) 2. Interactive Simulator Use the simulator below to visualize how charges alter the space around them. Add charges in 3D, switch to a 2D top view when useful, compare field lines with vector arrows, and notice that the probe arrow is just the field vector at one chosen point. 3. Understanding Field Lines The simulator above draws yellow curves through space. These are field lines . They are not physical strings, but they are useful because their direction matches the electric field direction at each point. 1. Direction Field lines always originate from Positive (+) charges and terminate on Negative (-) charges (or go to infinity). 2. Strength (Density) The density of the lines represents the strength of the field. Where lines are bunched close together (near the charge), the field is strong. Where they spread out, the field is weak. 3. The Tangent At any specific point on a curved line, the direction of the el",
