      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Derivation & The Curl Condition Home > Electrostatics > Electric Potential > Derivation & Curl Deep Dive: Derivation & The Curl Condition The formula for electric potential is not arbitrary. It is derived directly from the physical concepts of Work and Energy, subject to the mathematical condition that the field is conservative. 1. The Physical Origin: Work and Energy Imagine moving a test charge \\(Q\\) from point \\(a\\) to point \\(b\\). The electric force exerted on the charge is \\(\\vec{F} = Q\\vec{E}\\). The Work done by this force is: $$ W_{a \\to b} = \\int_a^b \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{l} = Q \\int_a^b \\vec{E} \\cdot d\\vec{l} $$ Because the electrostatic force is conservative , the work done is equal to the negative change in Potential Energy (\\(U\\)) . Think of lifting a rock against gravity: you do work, and the rock gains potential energy. $$ W_{a \\to b} = - \\Delta U = -(U_b - U_a) $$ The Distinction between \\(U\\) and \\(V\\): Potential Energy (\\(U\\)) depends on the size of the test charge \\(Q\\). To get a property of the field itself, we define Electric Potential (\\(V\\)) as the potential energy per unit charge : $$ V \\equiv \\frac{U}{Q} $$ Combining these equations, we get the fundamental relation for potential difference: $$ V_b - V_a = - \\int_a^b \\vec{E} \\cdot d\\vec{l} $$ 2. The Reference Point \\(\\mathcal{O}\\) The equation above only calculates the difference in potential. To define \\(V(\\vec{r})\\) as a specific number, we must pick a reference point \\(\\mathcal{O}\\) where we agree that \\(V=0\\). $$ V(\\vec{r}) = - \\int_{\\mathcal{O}}^{\\vec{r}} \\vec{E} \\cdot d\\vec{l} $$ Changing the Reference Point There is an essential ambiguity here. If we choose a different reference point \\(\\mathcal{O}'\\), we are essentially adding a constant \\(K\\) to the potential everywhere. $$ V'(\\v",
