      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Calculations & Examples Home > Electrostatics > Electric Potential > Examples Deep Dive: Calculations & Examples There are two primary ways to calculate the potential \\(V\\): Method 1: Integrate the Electric Field (if \\(\\vec{E}\\) is already known, e.g., via Gauss's Law). Method 2: Integrate the Charge Distribution (if \\(\\vec{E}\\) is unknown). 1. The Master Formulas If you know the Field \\(\\vec{E}\\): $$ V(\\vec{r}) = - \\int_{\\infty}^{\\vec{r}} \\vec{E} \\cdot d\\vec{l} $$ If you know the Charge Density \\(\\rho\\): $$ V(\\vec{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\int \\frac{\\rho(\\vec{r}')}{|\\vec{r} - \\vec{r}'|} d\\tau' $$ 2. Example: Spherical Shell Consider a spherical shell of radius \\(R\\) with uniform surface charge \\(\\sigma\\). We want to find the potential everywhere. Method 1: Integrating the Field From Gauss's Law, we know the field is: Outside (\\(r > R\\)): \\(\\vec{E} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q}{r^2} \\hat{r}\\) Inside (\\(r \\(\\vec{E} = 0\\) Step A: Outside For a point \\(r > R\\), we integrate from infinity: $$ V(r) = - \\int_{\\infty}^{r} \\left( \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q}{z^2} \\right) dz = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q}{r} $$ This is identical to the potential of a point charge. Step B: Inside For a point \\(r $$ V(r) = - \\int_{\\infty}^{R} \\vec{E}_{\\text{out}} \\cdot d\\vec{l} - \\int_{R}^{r} \\vec{E}_{\\text{in}} \\cdot d\\vec{l} $$ The first term gives \\(V(R)\\). The second term is zero because \\(\\vec{E}_{\\text{in}} = 0\\). $$ V(r) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q}{R} \\quad (\\text{Constant!}) $$ Key Insight: The potential inside is not zero. It is constant. It takes work to bring a charge from infinity to the surface \\(R\\), but no additional work to move it around inside the shell. 3. Example: Uniform Line Charge Consider a thin rod of length \\(2L\\) lying on the x-ax",
