      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Basic Conductor Properties Home > Electric Fields in Matter > Conductors > Basic Properties Deep Dive: Basic Conductor Properties Free charges move until the internal field is zero. When an external field is applied, mobile charges pile up on the surface and create an induced field that cancels the original field inside the conductor. Once E=0, Gauss's law gives rho=0 in the interior, any excess charge resides on the surface, the conductor is an equipotential, and the outside electric field is normal to the surface. A Gaussian pillbox gives E outside equals sigma over epsilon0 times n-hat.",
