      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Alignment of Polar Molecules Home > Fields in Matter > Polarization > Alignment Details Deep Dive: Alignment of Polar Molecules Unlike neutral atoms which must be stretched to create a dipole, polar molecules (like water) have a permanent built-in dipole moment \\(\\vec{p}\\). When placed in an electric field, the physics is governed by Torque and Temperature . 1. Torque on a Dipole Consider a dipole consisting of a charge \\(+q\\) and \\(-q\\) separated by a vector \\(\\vec{d}\\). In a uniform electric field \\(\\vec{E}\\): The force on \\(+q\\) is \\(\\vec{F}_+ = q\\vec{E}\\). The force on \\(-q\\) is \\(\\vec{F}_- = -q\\vec{E}\\). The net force is zero, so the molecule doesn't accelerate linearly. However, because the forces act at different points, they create a Torque or Mechanical Moment (\\(\\mathbf{M}\\)) that rotates the molecule. $$ \\mathbf{M} = (\\vec{r}_+ \\times \\vec{F}_+) + (\\vec{r}_- \\times \\vec{F}_-) = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E} $$ This torque acts to align the dipole moment \\(\\vec{p}\\) parallel to the field \\(\\vec{E}\\). 2. Potential Energy Rotation requires work. The work done to rotate the dipole against the electric field is stored as potential energy \\(U\\). $$ U = - \\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{E} = -pE \\cos \\theta $$ where \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between \\(\\vec{p}\\) and \\(\\vec{E}\\). Minimum Energy (\\(\\theta = 0\\)): Aligned with field. Stable equilibrium. Maximum Energy (\\(\\theta = \\pi\\)): Anti-aligned. Unstable equilibrium. 3. The Battle: Field vs. Heat If torque were the only factor, every molecule in a dielectric would line up perfectly with the field, creating massive polarization. In reality, this doesn't happen. Why? Thermal Agitation. At any temperature \\(T > 0\\), molecules are randomly colliding and rotating. This thermal energy (\\(k_B T\\)) tends to randomize the orientation",
