      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Wire with Insulation Home > Fields in Matter > The Electric Displacement > Example: Wire with Insulation Deep Dive: Wire with Insulation Problem: A long straight wire, carrying a uniform line charge \\(\\lambda\\), is surrounded by rubber insulation out to a radius \\(a\\). Find the electric displacement \\(\\vec{D}\\). 1. Applying Gauss's Law for D The beauty of the Electric Displacement is that it depends only on free charge . The rubber insulation will become polarized and create bound charges, but we can ignore them for now. We draw a cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius \\(s\\) and length \\(L\\). Because of the cylindrical symmetry, \\(\\vec{D}\\) must point radially outward: \\(\\vec{D} = D(s) \\hat{s}\\). Using Gauss's Law for Displacement: $$ \\oint \\vec{D} \\cdot d\\vec{a} = Q_{f,enc} $$ The total free charge enclosed by the cylinder is simply the charge on the wire (\\(\\lambda L\\)). The surface integral gives the area of the cylinder wall (\\(2\\pi s L\\)): $$ D(2\\pi s L) = \\lambda L $$ 2. The Result Solving for \\(\\vec{D}\\): $$ \\vec{D} = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi s} \\hat{s} $$ Crucially: This formula holds everywhere —both inside the insulation (\\(s a\\)). The symmetry of the free charge dictates \\(\\vec{D}\\) regardless of the material. 3. Finding the Electric Field While we know \\(\\vec{D}\\) everywhere, finding \\(\\vec{E}\\) requires knowing the relationship between \\(\\vec{D}\\) and \\(\\vec{E}\\) (which depends on polarization). Outside the Insulation (\\(s > a\\)) Outside, there is no material, so \\(\\vec{P} = 0\\). Since \\(\\vec{D} = \\epsilon_0 \\vec{E} + \\vec{P}\\), we simply have \\(\\vec{D} = \\epsilon_0 \\vec{E}\\). $$ \\vec{E} = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi \\epsilon_0 s} \\hat{s} \\quad (\\text{for } s > a) $$ Inside the Insulation (\\(s Inside the rubber, \\(\\vec{E}\\) cannot be determined yet because we",
