      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Energy in Dielectric Systems Home > Linear Dielectrics > Energy Derivation Deep Dive: Energy in Dielectric Systems When we calculate the energy of a configuration involving dielectrics, we must be careful. Are we counting the work done on all charges, or just the free charges we can physically manipulate? 1. The Capacitor Motivation Recall that the energy of a charged capacitor is: $$ W = \\frac{1}{2} C V^2 $$ When a linear dielectric is inserted, the capacitance increases: \\(C = \\epsilon_r C_{\\text{vac}}\\). This means for a fixed voltage, the energy stored increases by a factor of \\(\\epsilon_r\\). Physically, this is because we must add more free charge to maintain the same potential, as some of the field is canceled by the bound charges of the dielectric. 2. The Incremental Work Proof Suppose we have a dielectric material fixed in position, and we build up the free charge distribution \\(\\rho_f\\) a bit at a time. If we increase the free charge by an amount \\(\\Delta \\rho_f\\), the work done is: $$ \\Delta W = \\int (\\Delta \\rho_f) V \\, d\\tau $$ Since \\(\\div \\vec{D} = \\rho_f\\), the change in charge density is related to the change in displacement: \\(\\Delta \\rho_f = \\div (\\Delta \\vec{D})\\). $$ \\Delta W = \\int [ \\div (\\Delta \\vec{D}) ] V \\, d\\tau $$ Using the product rule \\(\\div [(\\Delta \\vec{D}) V] = [\\div (\\Delta \\vec{D})] V + \\Delta \\vec{D} \\cdot (\\grad V)\\) and integrating by parts: $$ \\Delta W = \\int \\div [(\\Delta \\vec{D}) V] \\, d\\tau + \\int (\\Delta \\vec{D}) \\cdot \\vec{E} \\, d\\tau $$ The first term vanishes if we integrate over all space (the surface integral at infinity goes to zero). Now, if the medium is a linear dielectric , then \\(\\vec{D} = \\epsilon \\vec{E}\\). For small increments: $$ \\Delta (\\vec{D} \\cdot \\vec{E}) = \\Delta (\\epsilon E^2) = \\epsilon \\Delta ",
