      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Derivation & Example Home > Fields in Matter > Field of Magnetized Object > Derivation & Example Deep Dive: Derivation & Example This page details the mathematical transformation from the dipole potential integral to the concept of Bound Currents. 1. Deriving Bound Currents To understand where the integral comes from, we must first visualize the coordinate system. We are summing up the contributions of infinitesimal dipoles \\(\\bfM d\\tau'\\) located at source points \\(\\vecrp\\). Step 1: The Potential Integral The vector potential \\(\\bfA\\) at observation point \\(\\vecr\\) is the sum (integral) of the potentials produced by dipoles in the volume: $$ \\bfA(\\vecr) = \\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi} \\int \\frac{\\bfM(\\vecrp) \\times \\rhat}{r^2} \\dtau $$ \\(\\vecr\\) : Observation point. \\(\\vecrp\\) : Source point inside the material. \\(r\\) : Separation distance \\(|\\vecr - \\vecrp|\\). \\(\\rhat\\) : Unit vector pointing from \\(\\vecrp\\) to \\(\\vecr\\). Step 2: Vector Calculus Identity We use the identity \\(\\nabla' (1/r) = \\rhat/r^2\\) to rewrite the integrand: $$ \\frac{\\bfM \\times \\rhat}{r^2} = \\bfM \\times \\left( \\nabla' \\frac{1}{r} \\right) $$ Using the product rule \\(\\nabla' \\times (f\\mathbf{v}) = f(\\nabla' \\times \\mathbf{v}) - \\mathbf{v} \\times \\nabla' f\\), we expand: $$ \\bfM \\times \\nabla' \\left(\\frac{1}{r}\\right) = \\frac{1}{r} (\\nabla' \\times \\bfM) - \\nabla' \\times \\left( \\frac{\\bfM}{r} \\right) $$ Step 3: Resulting Bound Currents Substituting this back into the integral, we use the Divergence Theorem on the second term to convert it to a surface integral: $$ \\bfA(\\vecr) = \\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi} \\int_V \\frac{\\nabla' \\times \\bfM}{r} \\dtau + \\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi} \\oint_S \\frac{\\bfM \\times \\nhat}{r} da' $$ Comparing this to the standard potentials for currents, we identify the terms: $$ \\bfJ_b = \\nabla \\ti",
