      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Deriving Ampère’s Law for H Home > Fields in Matter > The Auxiliary Field H > Derivation Deep Dive: Deriving Ampère’s Law for H The motivation for introducing \\(\\mathbf{H}\\) is to express magnetic fields in terms of Free Current (\\(\\mathbf{J}_f\\)) alone, hiding the complex bound currents (\\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\)) inside the material definition. 1. The Differential Derivation We start with the fundamental Ampère’s Law for the total magnetic field \\(\\mathbf{B}\\), which depends on all currents. Step 1: Total Current The total current density \\(\\mathbf{J}\\) is the sum of the free current (in wires) and bound current (from magnetization). $$ \\frac{1}{\\mu_0} (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B}) = \\mathbf{J} = \\mathbf{J}_f + \\mathbf{J}_b $$ Step 2: Substitute Bound Current Recall from the previous section that the bound current is the curl of the magnetization: \\(\\mathbf{J}_b = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M}\\). $$ \\frac{1}{\\mu_0} (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B}) = \\mathbf{J}_f + (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M}) $$ Step 3: Definition of H Move the magnetization term to the left side and group the curls: $$ \\nabla \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\mathbf{B} - \\mathbf{M} \\right) = \\mathbf{J}_f $$ The term in parentheses is defined as the Auxiliary Field, \\(\\mathbf{H}\\), leading to: $$ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{J}_f $$ 2. The Integral Form In practice, we rarely use the differential form directly. By applying Stokes' Theorem to a surface \\(S\\) bounded by a loop \\(P\\), we get the useful form for solving problems with symmetry: $$ \\oint \\mathbf{H} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = I_{f_{\\text{enc}}} $$ Note: This integral form is typically useful only in cases of high symmetry (such as infinite cylinders, solenoids, or planes) where \\(\\mathbf{H}\\) can be pulled out of the integral. 3. Worked Example: Long Copper Ro",
