      "searchText": "Deep Dive: The Copper Rod Home > Fields in Matter > Auxiliary Field H > Example: The Copper Rod Deep Dive: The Copper Rod This example demonstrates how to find the auxiliary field $\\bfH$ in a simple geometric configuration where free current is distributed throughout a material. Since $\\bfH$ depends only on the free current, it simplifies calculations immensely. The Problem: A long copper rod of radius $R$ carries a uniformly distributed (free) current $I$. Find $\\bfH$ inside and outside the rod. Solution Copper is weakly diamagnetic, so the dipoles will line up opposite to the field. This results in bound currents running antiparallel to $I$ within the wire, and parallel to $I$ along the surface. However, to calculate $\\bfH$, we only need to consider the free current. Inside the Wire ($s \\le R$) We use a circular Amperian loop of radius $s$. The enclosed free current is proportional to the area: $$ H(2\\pi s) = I_{f,enc} = I \\frac{\\pi s^2}{\\pi R^2} $$ $$ \\bfH = \\frac{I}{2\\pi R^2} s \\bfphi $$ Outside the Wire ($s \\ge R$) The total free current enclosed is simply $I$: $$ \\bfH = \\frac{I}{2\\pi s} \\bfphi $$ Note on B: Outside the wire (in empty space), $\\bfM = 0$, so $\\bfB = \\mu_0 \\bfH$. Inside the wire, $\\bfB$ cannot be determined at this stage without knowing the magnetization properties of copper. Why Use H instead of B? In the laboratory, $\\bfH$ is often a more useful quantity than $\\bfB$ for a very practical reason: Current. To build an electromagnet, you run a certain (free) current through a coil. This current is what you read on your dial, and it directly determines $\\bfH$ (specifically, the line integral of $\\bfH$). $\\bfB$ depends on the specific materials used (like iron cores) and even on the history of the magnet (hysteresis). This is the opposite of the situatio",
