      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions & Shielding Home > Fields in Matter > Linear Media > Boundary Conditions Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions & Shielding When magnetic fields pass from one material to another (e.g., from air to iron), the fields change abruptly. To solve problems involving interfaces, we derive Boundary Conditions from the integral forms of Maxwell's Equations. 1. The Rules of the Interface 1. Normal Component of B From the divergence law \\(\\nabla \\cdot \\bfB = 0\\), applying the Divergence Theorem to a small \"pillbox\" spanning the interface tells us that the magnetic flux entering must equal the flux leaving. $$ B_{1}^{\\perp} = B_{2}^{\\perp} $$ The perpendicular component of \\(\\bfB\\) is continuous . 2. Tangential Component of H From Ampere's Law for H: \\(\\oint \\bfH \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = I_{f,enc}\\). Applying this to a small loop straddling the interface, assuming no free surface current (\\(\\bfK_f = 0\\)): $$ H_{1}^{\\parallel} = H_{2}^{\\parallel} $$ The parallel component of \\(\\bfH\\) is continuous . These seemingly simple rules have profound consequences. Since \\(\\bfB = \\mu \\bfH\\), the condition \\(H_{1}^{\\parallel} = H_{2}^{\\parallel}\\) implies: $$ \\frac{B_{1}^{\\parallel}}{\\mu_1} = \\frac{B_{2}^{\\parallel}}{\\mu_2} \\implies B_{2}^{\\parallel} = \\frac{\\mu_2}{\\mu_1} B_{1}^{\\parallel} $$ If Material 2 is highly permeable iron (\\(\\mu_2 \\gg \\mu_1\\)), the parallel magnetic field inside the iron is huge! This \"sucks\" magnetic field lines into the material. 2. Example: Magnetic Shielding 🛡️ The Problem Suppose we have a hollow spherical shell of high-permeability iron (\\(\\mu\\)) placed in a uniform external magnetic field \\(\\bfB_0\\). What is the field inside the empty cavity? Step 1: The Potential Method Since there are no free currents, we can use the magnetic scalar potenti",
