      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Ferromagnetism: Domains & Hysteresis Home > Fields in Matter > Linear & Nonlinear Media > Ferromagnetism Details Deep Dive: Ferromagnetism: Domains & Hysteresis Why is a standard iron nail attracted to a magnet, but doesn't act like a magnet itself? Why does heating a magnet destroy it? The answers lie in the microscopic structure of the material. 1. Magnetic Domains In ferromagnetic materials, a quantum mechanical effect called the Exchange Interaction forces neighboring atomic dipoles to align parallel to each other. This creates regions of perfect alignment called Domains . The Energy Trade-off: Exchange Energy wants dipoles to align (parallel). Magnetostatic Energy wants dipoles to anti-align to cancel the external field. The compromise is to form domains. Within a domain, spins are parallel (Exchange wins). But neighboring domains point in different directions to cancel the net field (Magnetostatic wins). A standard piece of iron has randomly oriented domains, so the net magnetization \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is zero. When we apply an external field \\(\\mathbf{H}\\), two things happen: Domain Wall Motion: Domains aligned with the field grow, \"eating\" their neighbors. Domain Rotation: At high fields, the dipoles within the domains physically rotate to align with \\(\\mathbf{H}\\). 2. The Hysteresis Loop The alignment process is not perfectly reversible because domain walls get \"stuck\" on impurities in the crystal structure. This friction leads to Hysteresis (Greek for \"lagging behind\"). Saturation Point All domains are fully aligned. Increasing \\(\\mathbf{H}\\) further creates no additional magnetization. Retentivity (Remanence) When \\(\\mathbf{H}\\) is removed (back to 0), the domains relax slightly but do not return to random. The material retains a magnetization \\(\\mathb",
