      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Motional EMF Home > Electrodynamics > Electromotive Force > Motional EMF Deep Dive: Motional EMF Deriving the Flux Rule from the Lorentz Force In the main topic, we stated that $\\mathcal{E} = vBh$ for a bar moving through a magnetic field. Here, we show why this is true, starting only from the Lorentz force law. Visualization 1. The Lorentz Force on Charges We start with the fundamental Lorentz force law. When a charge $q$ moves with velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ in a magnetic field $\\mathbf{B}$, it experiences a force: $$ \\mathbf{F}_{mag} = q(\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}) $$ Now, consider a conducting bar of length $h$ moving with velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ perpendicular to both the bar and a uniform field $\\mathbf{B}$. The free charges inside the bar experience this force will separate and accumulate at the ends. 2. The EMF as Work per Unit Charge The EMF is defined as the work done per unit charge by the non-electrostatic force around the circuit. For the moving bar, this is provided by the magnetic Lorentz force: $$ \\mathcal{E} = \\oint (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} $$ For a straight bar of length $h$ moving with speed $v$ perpendicular to $\\mathbf{B}$: $$ \\mathcal{E} = \\int_0^h (vB) \\, dl = vBh $$ This is exactly the formula stated in the main topic. 3. Connecting to the Flux Rule When the bar moves a distance $dx$ in time $dt$, it \"sweeps out\" an area $dA = h \\cdot dx$. The change in magnetic flux through the loop is: $$ d\\Phi = B \\cdot dA = Bh \\, dx $$ The rate of change of flux is: $$ \\frac{d\\Phi}{dt} = Bh \\frac{dx}{dt} = Bhv $$ Comparing with our earlier result $\\mathcal{E} = vBh$: $$ \\mathcal{E} = -\\frac{d\\Phi}{dt} $$ This is Faraday's Law . The minus sign (Lenz's Law) indicates the induced EMF opposes the change in flux. 4. Key Insight: Phase ",
