      "searchText": "Deep Dive: The Drude Model Home > Electrodynamics > Electromotive Force > Drude Model Deep Dive: The Drude Model Understanding the Microscopic Origin of Resistance Ohm's Law ($\\mathbf{J} = \\sigma \\mathbf{E}$) is not a fundamental law of nature like Maxwell’s equations. Instead, it is an empirical description of how certain materials behave. To understand why it works, we must look at the behavior of electrons at the microscopic level. 1. The Drude Model Assumptions The Drude model treats a conductor as a 3D lattice of heavy, stationary ions with a \"gas\" of free electrons moving between them. It relies on a few key classical assumptions: Free Electron Gas: Electrons move freely until they hit an ion. Instantaneous Collisions: Collisions are sudden events that randomize the electron's velocity. Mean Free Time ($\\tau$): There is an average time interval between collisions, during which the electron accelerates under an external field. 2. Thermal Motion vs. Drift Velocity Electrons are never still; they move randomly at high speeds ($v_{th} \\approx 10^6$ m/s) even without a field. This random motion contributes zero net flow. When an external field $\\mathbf{E}$ is applied, it exerts a force $\\mathbf{F} = -e\\mathbf{E}$. Under this force, electrons constantly accelerate between collisions. However, because they are constantly colliding with the lattice—effectively resetting their velocity—they reach a steady state \"average\" called the drift velocity ($\\mathbf{v}_d$). Interactive: Adjust the conductivity to see how the collision frequency ($\\tau$) affects the net flow ($J$). Note how ions glow red as they absorb energy during collisions. 3. Deriving Ohm's Law Between collisions, an electron accelerates according to Newton's Second Law: $$ \\mathbf{F} = m\\mathbf{a} = -e\\mathbf{E",
