      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Why Current Cannot Pile Up Home > Electrodynamics > Electromotive Force > Current at a Junction. Current is charge per second. At a circuit junction, currents entering a small node add charge and currents leaving remove charge. The stored node charge changes according to dQ_node/dt = I_in - I_top - I_bottom. In an ordinary steady circuit, the node may hold a tiny surface charge, but it does not store a growing amount of charge, so dQ_node/dt = 0 and I_in = I_top + I_bottom. If the currents do not balance, charge accumulates or drains at the node and the electric field adjusts the current distribution.",
