      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Mutual & Self Inductance Home > Electrodynamics > Electromagnetic Induction > Inductance Deep Dive: Mutual & Self Inductance Because the magnetic field is proportional to the current that creates it, the flux through any nearby loop must also be proportional to that current. This proportionality constant encapsulates all the complex geometry into a single number: Inductance . 1. Mutual Inductance ($M$) Consider two loops of wire. Current $I_1$ flowing in the first loop produces a magnetic field $\\vec{B}_1$. A portion of these field lines passes through the second loop, creating a magnetic flux $\\Phi_2$. Calculating $\\vec{B}_1$ from the Biot-Savart law is hard. But we know one crucial fact: the field is proportional to the current. Therefore: $$ \\Phi_2 = M_{21} I_1 $$ The constant $M_{21}$ is the Mutual Inductance of the two loops. It depends only on their shapes, sizes, and relative positions. The Neumann Formula By expressing the flux in terms of the vector potential $\\vec{A}_1$, one can derive an elegant formula that makes a remarkable fact obvious: $$ M_{21} = \\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi} \\oint_{loop1} \\oint_{loop2} \\frac{d\\vec{l}_1 \\cdot d\\vec{l}_2}{|\\vec{r}_1 - \\vec{r}_2|} $$ The integral is symmetric under swapping loop 1 and loop 2. Therefore: $$ M_{21} = M_{12} \\equiv M $$ This means: the flux through loop 2 when we run current $I$ through loop 1 is identical to the flux through loop 1 when we run the same current $I$ through loop 2. We can drop the subscripts and simply call it $M$. 2. Self Inductance ($L$) A changing current in loop 1 doesn't just induce an EMF in nearby loop 2—it also induces an EMF in itself . The flux through a loop due to its own current is: $$ \\Phi = L I $$ Where $L$ is the Self Inductance (or simply \"inductance\"). Origin of the Back EMF",
