      "searchText": "Deep Dive: The Flaw in Ampère's Law Home > Electrodynamics > Maxwell's Equations > History Deep Dive: The Flaw in Ampère's Law Before Maxwell, Ampère's Law was written as $\\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\vec{J}$. Maxwell noticed a fatal mathematical contradiction in this equation for non-steady currents. The Mathematical Contradiction Take the divergence of the curl of any vector field. It must be zero (vector identity): $$ \\nabla \\cdot (\\nabla \\times \\vec{B}) = 0 $$ However, taking the divergence of the right side of Ampère's Law gives: $$ \\nabla \\cdot (\\mu_0 \\vec{J}) = \\mu_0 (\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{J}) $$ From the continuity equation (conservation of charge), $\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{J} = -\\frac{\\partial \\rho}{\\partial t}$. This is only zero for steady currents ($\\dot{\\rho} = 0$). If charge is piling up (like on a capacitor plate), the equation breaks! $$ 0 \\neq -\\mu_0 \\dot{\\rho} \\quad \\text{(Contradiction!)} $$",
      "snippet": "Deep Dive: The Flaw in Ampère's Law Home > Electrodynamics > Maxwell's Equations > History Deep Dive: The Flaw in Ampère's Law Before Maxwell, Ampère's Law was written as $\\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\vec{J}$. Maxwell "
