      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Polarization Home > EM Waves > Waves in Vacuum > Polarization Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: Polarization The Polarization Vector The polarization of an EM wave is defined by the direction of the electric field vector $\\vec{E}$. We characterize this using a complex displacement vector $\\vec{n}$ (the polarization vector). For a plane wave: $$ \\tilde{\\mathbf{E}}(z, t) = \\tilde{E}_0 e^{i(kz-\\omega t)} \\hat{n} $$ If $\\hat{n}$ is a real unit vector, the wave is linearly polarized . If $\\hat{n}$ is complex, the tip of $\\vec{E}$ traces out an ellipse. Types of Polarization Linear The E-field oscillates in a single fixed direction. The polarization vector $\\hat{n}$ is real. Circular The E-field tip traces a circle. Two orthogonal waves with equal amplitude and $90^\\circ$ phase shift. Elliptical The most general case where amplitudes are unequal or the phase shift is not $90^\\circ$. Handedness Convention Looking towards the source, if the vector rotates clockwise, it is Right-Handed (RHCP). Using the $(\\hat{x} + i\\hat{y})$ convention corresponds to a specific rotation direction that depends on whether you choose $kz - \\omega t$ or $\\omega t - kz$ in the exponent. Mathematical Descriptions Linear A wave polarized at an angle $\\theta$ to the $x$-axis: $$ \\vec{E}(z,t) = E_0 \\cos(kz - \\omega t) (\\cos\\theta \\hat{x} + \\sin\\theta \\hat{y}) $$ Circular Two orthogonal waves with a \\(90^\\circ\\) phase shift: $$ \\tilde{\\mathbf{E}}_{R/L} = E_0 e^{i(kz-\\omega t)} (\\hat{x} \\pm i\\hat{y}) $$ In real terms: $$ \\vec{E}(z,t) = E_0 [\\cos(kz-\\omega t)\\hat{x} \\mp \\sin(kz-\\omega t)\\hat{y}] $$",
      "snippet": "Deep Dive: Polarization Home > EM Waves > Waves in Vacuum > Polarization Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: Polarization The Polarization Vector The polarization of an EM wave is defined by the direction of the electric field vector "
