      "searchText": "Deep Dive: The Wave Equation Home > EM Waves > Waves in Vacuum > Wave Equation Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: The Wave Equation Derivation from Maxwell's Equations In a source-free vacuum ($\\rho = 0, \\mathbf{J} = 0$), we start with Maxwell's Equations: $$ (i) \\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E} = 0 \\quad (ii) \\nabla \\cdot \\vec{B} = 0 $$ $$ (iii) \\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = -\\pd{\\vec{B}}{t} \\quad (iv) \\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\pd{\\vec{E}}{t} $$ To decouple these, take the curl of Faraday's Law (iii): $$ \\nabla \\times (\\nabla \\times \\vec{E}) = \\nabla \\times \\left(-\\pd{\\vec{B}}{t}\\right) = -\\pd{}{t} (\\nabla \\times \\vec{B}) $$ Applying the vector identity $\\nabla \\times (\\nabla \\times \\vec{A}) = \\nabla(\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{A}) - \\nabla^2 \\vec{A}$ to the left side: $$ \\nabla(\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E}) - \\nabla^2 \\vec{E} = -\\pd{}{t} \\left( \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\pd{\\vec{E}}{t} \\right) $$ Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E} = 0$ in vacuum, the first term vanishes, leaving: $$ \\nabla^2 \\vec{E} = \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial^2 \\vec{E}}{\\partial t^2} $$ This matches the standard 3D wave equation $\\nabla^2 f = \\frac{1}{v^2}\\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial t^2}$ where the speed is $c = 1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0} \\approx 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s. The Transverse Nature Consider a monochromatic plane wave propagating in the $z$-direction: $$ \\tilde{\\mathbf{E}}(z, t) = \\tilde{\\mathbf{E}}_0 e^{i(kz - \\omega t)}, \\quad \\tilde{\\mathbf{B}}(z, t) = \\tilde{\\mathbf{B}}_0 e^{i(kz - \\omega t)} $$ Applying $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = 0$ (Gauss's Law): $$ \\frac{\\partial E_x}{\\partial x} + \\frac{\\partial E_y}{\\partial y} + \\frac{\\partial E_z}{\\partial z} = 0 \\implies i k \\tilde{E}_{0z} e^{i(kz-\\omega t)} = 0 \\implies \\tilde{E}_{0z} = 0 $$ This proves that electromagnetic waves are transverse : the field oscillates perpendicular to the dire",
      "snippet": "Deep Dive: The Wave Equation Home > EM Waves > Waves in Vacuum > Wave Equation Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: The Wave Equation Derivation from Maxwell's Equations In a source-free vacuum ($\\rho = 0, \\mathbf{J} = 0$), we start wi"
