      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Mathematics of Waves Deep Dive: Mathematics of Waves The 1-D Wave Equation A wave is any disturbance \\(f(x,t)\\) that preserves its shape as it moves. The most general form of such a wave is governed by the 1D partial differential equation: $$ \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} = \\frac{1}{v^2} \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial t^2} $$ The General Solution: d'Alembert proved that any function of the form \\(f(x,t) = g(x - vt) + h(x + vt)\\) satisfies this equation. This means any shape—square pulses, pulses shaped like Gaussian bells, or complex signals—can be a wave if its time-evolution is coupled to its spatial position in this specific way. Extending to 3D In three dimensions, the disturbance may depend on \\(\\vec{r} = (x, y, z)\\). The scalar wave equation then uses the Laplacian operator (\\(\\nabla^2\\)): $$ \\nabla^2 f = \\frac{1}{v^2} \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial t^2} $$ Where \\(\\nabla^2 f = \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z^2}\\). Solutions to this equation include Plane Waves (spanning all space in two directions) and Spherical Waves (which decay as \\(1/r\\) from a point source). Interactive: The 1D Wave Equation Visualization of a sinusoidal traveling wave. Adjust parameters to see how frequency and wavelength relate to speed. Sinusoidal Waves While the wave equation allows for any function \\(f(x,t) = g(x-vt)\\), the most practical and mathematically significant solutions are Sinusoidal Waves . They serve as the fundamental building blocks for all other waves via Fourier Analysis. $$ f(x, t) = A \\cos(kx - \\omega t + \\delta) = \\text{Re}\\{ \\tilde{A} e^{i(kx - \\omega t)} \\} $$ Here, \\(\\tilde{A} = Ae^{i\\delta}\\) is the complex amplitude , which simplifies calculations by packing both the physical amplit",
