      "searchText": "Ch 6.2: Energy & Momentum Home > EM Waves > Energy & Momentum Energy and Momentum 1. The Poynting Vector Electromagnetic waves transport energy. The energy per unit time, per unit area, transported by the fields is given by the Poynting vector , $\\mathbf{S}$: $$ \\mathbf{S} = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0} (\\mathbf{E} \\times \\mathbf{B}) $$ As waves propagate through media (conductors), they lose energy, and their amplitude decays. This is often characterized by the \"skin depth\". + Deep Dive: The Poynting Vector 2. Radiation Pressure Electromagnetic waves also carry momentum. When a wave strikes a surface, it transfers this momentum, exerting a force known as radiation pressure. For complete absorption, pressure $P = I/c$. For perfect reflection, $P = 2I/c$. + Deep Dive: Radiation Pressure",
