      "searchText": "Deep Dive: The Poynting Vector Home > EM Waves > Energy & Momentum > Poynting Vector Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: The Poynting Vector Poynting's Theorem: Derivation How much work does an EM field do on a collection of charges? The rate of work per unit volume is given by the power density: $$ \\vec{f} \\cdot \\vec{v} = \\rho(\\vec{E} + \\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}) \\cdot \\vec{v} = \\vec{J}\\cdot\\vec{E} $$ From Ampère's Law (with Maxwell's correction), we solve for current density $\\vec{J}$: $$ \\vec{J} = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\curl\\vec{B} - \\epsilon_0\\pd{\\vec{E}}{t} $$ Substituting this into the power equation: $$ \\vec{J}\\cdot\\vec{E} = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0}(\\curl\\vec{B})\\cdot\\vec{E} - \\epsilon_0\\vec{E}\\cdot\\pd{\\vec{E}}{t} $$ Using the vector product rule $\\div(\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}) = \\vec{B}\\cdot(\\curl\\vec{E}) - \\vec{E}\\cdot(\\curl\\vec{B})$ and Faraday's Law ($\\curl\\vec{E} = -\\pd{\\vec{B}}{t}$): $$ \\vec{J}\\cdot\\vec{E} = -\\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\div(\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}) - \\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\vec{B}\\cdot\\pd{\\vec{B}}{t} - \\epsilon_0\\vec{E}\\cdot\\pd{\\vec{E}}{t} $$ Recognizing that $\\vec{A}\\cdot\\pd{\\vec{A}}{t} = \\frac{1}{2}\\pd{A^2}{t}$, we obtain the integral form of Poynting's Theorem : $$ \\frac{dW}{dt} = -\\frac{d}{dt} \\int_V \\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\epsilon_0 E^2 + \\frac{1}{\\mu_0} B^2 \\right) d\\tau - \\oint_S \\vec{S} \\cdot d\\vec{a} $$ Conservation of Energy: The work done per unit time on the charges ($dW/dt$) equals the rate at which field energy decreases minus the rate at which energy flows out through the bounding surface. Intensity For sinusoidal waves, the rapid fluctuations of $\\mathbf{S}$ are usually averaged out. The average power per unit area, called intensity ($I$), is: $$ I = \\langle S \\rangle = \\frac{1}{2} c \\epsilon_0 E_0^2 $$",
