      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Radiation Pressure Home > EM Waves > Energy & Momentum > Radiation Pressure Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: Radiation Pressure Momentum in Fields Just as fields store energy, they also store momentum . This is a crucial realization: momentum is not exclusive to matter! The momentum density $\\vec{g}$ (momentum per unit volume) is given by: $$ \\vec{g} = \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\vec{S} = \\frac{1}{c^2} \\vec{S} = \\epsilon_0 (\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}) $$ The total momentum stored in a volume $V$ is $\\vec{p}_{field} = \\int_V \\vec{g} \\, d\\tau$. If the field configuration changes, momentum is transferred to the charges in that volume, satisfying a conservation law analogous to Poynting's Theorem. Radiation Pressure Cases When a wave strikes a surface, it transfers its momentum. From Newton's Second Law, the force is the rate of momentum transfer ($\\vec{F} = d\\vec{p}/dt$). The pressure $P$ is this force per unit area: $P = I/c$ Perfect Absorption . The surface \"stops\" the light, absorbing $100\\%$ of its momentum. $P = \\langle g \\rangle c = I/c$. $P = 2I/c$ Perfect Reflection . The surface reverses the direction of the light. The momentum change is $\\Delta p = p_{final} - p_{initial} = (-p) - p = -2p$, doubling the pressure. Note: In most everyday scenarios, radiation pressure is incredibly weak (a few micronewtons per square meter), yet it is sufficient to push comet tails away from the sun. Advance Concept: The Maxwell Stress Tensor In general, the force on a surface isn't always from a simple plane wave hitting it head-on. Physicists use the Maxwell Stress Tensor ($T_{ij}$) to calculate the total force on a volume by integrating over its surface: $$ \\vec{F}_{total} = \\oint_S \\overleftrightarrow{\\mathbf{T}} \\cdot d\\vec{a} - \\frac{d}{dt} \\int_V \\vec{g} \\, d\\tau $$ The tensor $T_{ij}$ rep",
