      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions & Reflection Home > EM Waves > Waves in Matter > Reflection Turin Prepnik Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions & Reflection The Four Boundary Conditions To determine what happens at a boundary between two media, we apply Maxwell's equations in integral form to a tiny \"pillbox\" or a thin loop at the interface. This gives us the general boundary conditions: $$ \\begin{align} 1. \\quad & \\epsilon_1 E_{1\\perp} - \\epsilon_2 E_{2\\perp} = \\sigma_f \\\\ 2. \\quad & B_{1\\perp} - B_{2\\perp} = 0 \\\\ 3. \\quad & \\vec{E}_{1\\parallel} - \\vec{E}_{2\\parallel} = 0 \\\\ 4. \\quad & \\frac{1}{\\mu_1} \\vec{B}_{1\\parallel} - \\frac{1}{\\mu_2} \\vec{B}_{2\\parallel} = \\vec{K}_f \\times \\hat{n} \\end{align} $$ For linear, non-conducting media ($\\sigma_f=0, K_f=0$), the tangential components of $\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{H}$ must be continuous. Derivation for Normal Incidence Suppose a wave polarized in the $x$-direction strikes a boundary (the $xy$-plane at $z=0$). The incident, reflected, and transmitted waves are: $$ \\tilde{\\vec{E}}_I = \\tilde{E}_{0I} e^{i(k_1 z - \\omega t)}\\hat{x}, \\quad \\tilde{\\vec{E}}_R = \\tilde{E}_{0R} e^{i(-k_1 z - \\omega t)}\\hat{x}, \\quad \\tilde{\\vec{E}}_T = \\tilde{E}_{0T} e^{i(k_2 z - \\omega t)}\\hat{x} $$ From the continuity of $\\vec{E}_{\\parallel}$ at $z=0$: $$ \\tilde{E}_{0I} + \\tilde{E}_{0R} = \\tilde{E}_{0T} $$ From the continuity of $\\vec{H}_{\\parallel}$ (assuming $\\mu_1 \\approx \\mu_2 \\approx \\mu_0$): $$ \\frac{1}{v_1} \\tilde{E}_{0I} - \\frac{1}{v_1} \\tilde{E}_{0R} = \\frac{1}{v_2} \\tilde{E}_{0T} \\implies n_1 (\\tilde{E}_{0I} - \\tilde{E}_{0R}) = n_2 \\tilde{E}_{0T} $$ Solving this system of two equations yields the Fresnel Equations for normal incidence: $$ \\tilde{E}_{0R} = \\left( \\frac{n_1 - n_2}{n_1 + n_2} \\right) \\tilde{E}_{0I}, \\quad \\tilde{E}_{0T} = \\left( \\frac{2n_1",
