      "searchText": "Deep Dive: How Maxwell Fixed Ampère's Law Home > Electrodynamics > Maxwell's Equations > Fixing Ampère Deep Dive: How Maxwell Fixed Ampère's Law 1. The Mathematical Contradiction Before Maxwell, Ampère's Law was written as $\\curl \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\vec{J}$. While this worked perfectly for steady currents (magnetostatics), Maxwell noticed a fatal mathematical flaw for time-varying currents. There is a vector calculus identity that states the divergence of a curl is always zero : $$ \\div (\\curl \\vec{A}) = 0 $$ If we apply the divergence to both sides of the old Ampère's Law: Left Side: $\\div (\\curl \\vec{B}) = 0$ (Always) Right Side: $\\div (\\mu_0 \\vec{J}) = \\mu_0 (\\div \\vec{J})$ However, the Continuity Equation (conservation of charge) states that $\\div \\vec{J} = -\\frac{\\partial \\rho}{\\partial t}$. If charge density is changing (like a charging capacitor), the right side is not zero . Conclusion: Ampère's Law $\\curl \\vec{B} = \\mu_0 \\vec{J}$ is mathematically impossible if charge density varies with time. 2. Maxwell's Correction Maxwell needed to add a term to the right side such that the divergence would essentially \"cancel out\" the continuity equation. We know from Gauss's Law that $\\rho = \\epsilon_0 (\\div \\vec{E})$. We can substitute this into the Continuity Equation: $$ \\begin{align} \\div \\vec{J} &= -\\frac{\\partial \\rho}{\\partial t} \\\\ &= -\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t} (\\epsilon_0 \\div \\vec{E}) \\\\ &= - \\div \\left( \\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t} \\right) \\end{align} $$ Moving everything to one side: $$ \\div \\left( \\vec{J} + \\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t} \\right) = 0 $$ This quantity inside the parentheses has a divergence of zero, which is exactly what is required to be the source of a curl. Thus, Maxwell amended Ampère's Law to: $$ \\curl \\vec{",
