      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Magnetic Monopoles & Symmetry Home > Electrodynamics > Maxwell's Equations > Symmetry Deep Dive: Magnetic Monopoles & Symmetry 1. The Missing Terms In a vacuum where $\\rho$ and $\\vec{J}$ are zero, Maxwell's equations are perfectly symmetric. However, in the presence of sources, they look lopsided. Electric fields have point sources (electric charges, $\\rho_e$). Magnetic fields do not; magnetic field lines always close on themselves ($\\div \\vec{B} = 0$). This lack of \"Magnetic Charge\" (Monopoles) breaks the aesthetic symmetry of the universe. 2. If Monopoles Existed... If nature allowed for magnetic charge density ($\\rho_m$) and magnetic current density ($\\vec{J}_m$), Maxwell's equations would become perfectly symmetrical pairs. Standard World $$ \\begin{align} \\div \\vec{E} &= \\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0}\\rho_e \\\\ \\div \\vec{B} &= 0 \\\\ \\curl \\vec{E} &= -\\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t} \\\\ \\curl \\vec{B} &= \\mu_0 \\vec{J}_e + \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t} \\end{align} $$ Symmetrical World $$ \\begin{align} \\div \\vec{E} &= \\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0}\\rho_e \\\\ \\div \\vec{B} &= \\mu_0 \\class{added-term}{\\rho_m} \\\\ \\curl \\vec{E} &= -\\class{added-term}{\\mu_0 \\vec{J}_m} -\\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t} \\\\ \\curl \\vec{B} &= \\mu_0 \\vec{J}_e + \\mu_0 \\epsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t} \\end{align} $$ In this theoretical framework: $\\rho_m$ represents \"North\" (+) or \"South\" (-) magnetic charges. $\\vec{J}_m$ represents the flow of these magnetic charges. The term $-\\mu_0 \\vec{J}_m$ in Faraday's law would mean a flow of magnetic monopoles generates an electric field directly. 3. The Duality Transformation Maxwell's equations exhibit a property called Duality . If you rotate the fields and sources by an angle $\\alpha$: $$ \\begin{aligned} \\vec{E}' &= \\vec{E} ",
