      "searchText": "Chapter 1: The Electric Field Home > Electrostatics > Chapter 1: The Electric Field Chapter 1: The Electric Field The fundamental problem of electrostatics is to determine the force acting on a specific charge due to a configuration of other stationary charges. This requires a precise understanding of how charges interact across space. 1. The Interaction of Two Charges Consider a single Source Charge (\\(q\\)) situated at position \\(\\vec{r}'\\) and a Test Charge (\\(Q\\)) situated at \\(\\vec{r}\\). The force exerted on the test charge is defined by Coulomb's Law : $$ \\vec{F} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q Q}{\\mathcal{r}^2} \\hat{\\mathcal{r}} $$ \\(q\\) The Source Charge . The charge creating the force. \\(Q\\) The Test Charge . The charge experiencing the force. \\(\\epsilon_0\\) Permittivity of Free Space . A physical constant \\(\\approx 8.85 \\times 10^{-12} \\, \\text{C}^2/\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}^2\\). \\(\\vec{\\mathcal{r}}\\) The Separation Vector \\(\\vec{\\mathcal{r}} = \\vec{r} - \\vec{r}'\\). It points from the source location to the test location. \\(\\mathcal{r}\\) The magnitude (distance) between the charges. \\(\\hat{\\mathcal{r}}\\) The unit vector defined as \\(\\frac{\\vec{\\mathcal{r}}}{|\\vec{\\mathcal{r}}|}\\). This specifies the direction of the force purely. ⚓ Deeper Dive: Constants & Historical Context 2. The Principle of Superposition In most physical situations, there is more than one source charge. Experiments confirm that the electromagnetic force obeys the Principle of Superposition . The total force on a test charge \\(Q\\) is the vector sum of the individual forces exerted by each source charge \\(q_i\\): $$ \\vec{F}_{net} = \\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2 + \\dots + \\vec{F}_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\vec{F}_i $$ 3. The Electric Field If we examine the superposition equation, we observe that the test charge",
