      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions Home > Magnetostatics > Vector Potential > Boundary Conditions Deep Dive: Boundary Conditions Discontinuities in $\\mathbf{B}$ and the continuity of $\\mathbf{A}$ Just as the electric field suffers a discontinuity at a surface charge, the magnetic field is discontinuous at a surface current. However, while the normal component of $\\mathbf{E}$ is discontinuous, for $\\mathbf{B}$ it is the tangential component that changes. 1. The Magnetostatic Triangle To understand boundary conditions, we first review the connections between current density $\\mathbf{J}$, magnetic field $\\mathbf{B}$, and vector potential $\\mathbf{A}$. Hover over the arrows to see the equations. This diagram reveals the \"missing link\" in magnetostatics. While we often calculate $\\mathbf{B}$ from $\\mathbf{A}$ via curl, calculating $\\mathbf{A}$ from $\\mathbf{B}$ is rarely required in standard problems. 2. Boundary Conditions for $\\mathbf{B}$ Consider a surface carrying a current density $\\mathbf{K}$. We apply Maxwell's equations in integral form to a small pillbox and an Amperian loop straddling the surface. Normal Component Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0$, the net flux through a wafer-thin pillbox is zero: $$\\oint \\mathbf{B} \\cdot d\\mathbf{a} = 0 \\implies B_{above}^\\perp - B_{below}^\\perp = 0$$ $$B_{above}^\\perp = B_{below}^\\perp$$ The normal component of the magnetic field is always continuous. Tangential Component Ampère's law ($\\oint \\mathbf{B} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\mu_0 I_{enc}$) applied to a rectangular loop perpendicular to the current yields: $$(B_{above}^\\parallel - B_{below}^\\parallel)l = \\mu_0 K l$$ $$B_{above}^\\parallel - B_{below}^\\parallel = \\mu_0 K$$ The tangential component is discontinuous by an amount proportional to the surface current. Combined into a singl",
