      "searchText": "Deep Dive: The Magnetic Dipole Home > Fields in Matter > Deep Dive: The Magnetic Dipole Deep Dive: The Magnetic Dipole In electrostatics, you learned that a \"Dipole\" consists of two opposite charges ($+q$ and $-q$) separated by a distance. In magnetostatics, we don't have magnetic monopoles (no isolated \"North\" charge). So, what is the fundamental unit of magnetism? 1. Defining the Magnetic Dipole The Fundamental Unit: The simplest magnetic object is not a pole, but a current loop . Any closed loop of current acts exactly like a tiny bar magnet. It has a \"North\" side (where field lines exit) and a \"South\" side (where field lines enter). Because it has these two poles, we call it a Magnetic Dipole . The Definition ($\\vec{m}$) We define a vector $\\vec{m}$, called the Magnetic Dipole Moment , to represent the strength and orientation of this loop. $$ \\vec{m} = I \\vec{A} $$ $I$ : The current flowing in the loop. $\\vec{A}$ : The area vector. Its magnitude is the area of the loop, and its direction is normal to the surface. Direction (Right-Hand Rule): Curl your fingers in the direction of the current $I$. Your thumb points in the direction of $\\vec{m}$ (and the North pole). 2. Interactive Mechanics Why does this definition matter? Because it allows us to calculate how magnetic fields interact with loops using simple vector math. Explore the simulation below to see the forces and torques in action. Tip: Use the \"Inspect: Right-Hand Rule\" button to see the local vectors. 3. Proof: Zero Net Force (Uniform Field) You might expect a magnet to be pulled by a magnetic field. However, in a uniform field, a pure dipole experiences no net force . The total force is the integral of the Lorentz force along the wire: $$ \\vec{F} = \\oint I (d\\vec{l} \\times \\vec{B}) $$ If $\\vec{B}$ is const",
