      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Magnetic Field of a Straight Wire Home > Magnetostatics > Biot-Savart Law > Infinite Straight Wire Deep Dive: Magnetic Field of a Straight Wire Problem: Find the magnetic field a distance $s$ from a long straight wire carrying a steady current $I$. I P s dl' r θ 1. Setup the Biot-Savart Law We begin with the general formula. The wire is aligned along the z-axis. The direction of the field, determined by the cross product $d\\mathbf{l}' \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$, is circumferential (into the page at point P). Thus, we only need to integrate the magnitude. $$ B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi} \\int \\frac{d\\mathbf{l}' \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{r}}}{r^2} $$ The magnitude of the cross product is $|d\\mathbf{l}'| \\sin(\\alpha)$, where $\\alpha$ is the angle between the current and the displacement vector. Looking at the geometry, if we define the angle $\\theta$ from the horizontal $s$, then $\\alpha = 90^\\circ + \\theta$, and $\\sin(90^\\circ+\\theta) = \\cos(\\theta)$. $$ dB = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi} \\frac{dz \\cos\\theta}{r^2} $$ 2. Trigonometric Substitution We have three variables changing as we integrate along the wire: $z$, $r$, and $\\theta$. It is easiest to express everything in terms of the angle $\\theta$. From the triangle in the diagram: 1. Relate distance $r$ to $s$: $$ r = \\frac{s}{\\cos\\theta} $$ 2. Relate height $z$ to $s$: $$ z = s \\tan\\theta $$ 3. Differentiate $z$ to get $dz$: $$ dz = s \\sec^2\\theta \\, d\\theta = \\frac{s}{\\cos^2\\theta} d\\theta $$ 3. Solving the Integral Substitute these terms back into the Biot-Savart expression: $$ B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi} \\int \\frac{\\left( \\frac{s}{\\cos^2\\theta} d\\theta \\right) \\cos\\theta}{\\left( \\frac{s}{\\cos\\theta} \\right)^2} $$ Simplifying the fraction: $$ B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi} \\int \\frac{s \\cos\\theta}{s^2} d\\theta = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi s} ",
