      "searchText": "Deep Dive: Work Done by Magnetic Forces Home > Magnetostatics > Work Done by Magnetic Forces Deep Dive: Work Done by Magnetic Forces The Work-Energy Theorem and Magnetic Forces It is a peculiar and fundamental property of the magnetic force that it does no work on a moving charged particle. At first glance, this assertion often provokes skepticism—after all, magnetic cranes lift cars, and electric motors drive machinery. However, if we examine the fundamental definition of work alongside the Lorentz force law, the conclusion is inescapable. Let us consider a particle of charge Q moving with velocity v through a magnetic field B . The magnetic force exerted on this particle is given by the cross product: $$ \\mathbf{F}_{\\text{mag}} = Q(\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}) $$ Mathematical Derivation Recall that the infinitesimal work dW done by a force F acting on a particle as it moves by a displacement d l is defined as the dot product of the force and the displacement: $$ dW = \\mathbf{F}_{\\text{mag}} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} $$ Because the particle is moving, we can express the infinitesimal displacement in terms of its velocity and the infinitesimal time interval dt , such that \\( d\\mathbf{l} = \\mathbf{v} \\, dt \\). Substituting the Lorentz force expression into our work equation, we obtain: $$ dW = [Q(\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B})] \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\, dt) $$ We can rearrange the scalar terms to highlight the vector operations. The crucial step relies on the geometric definition of the cross product. The vector \\( (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}) \\) is, by definition, perpendicular to both v and B . Consequently, the dot product of this vector with v must be zero. $$ dW = Q [(\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}) \\cdot \\mathbf{v}] \\, dt = 0 $$ Since the derivative of the work is zero, the tot",
